PRIVATE REFLECTION: { 
Current leaning: **34 States** is the minimum if the coalition may use aggressive but textually legal Senate tactics. Key corrections from round 1: the 51-month worst-case window from Jan. 1, 2000 ends Apr. 2004, so the 2004 Senate election is unavailable; only 2000 and 2002 elections can be used. Because only 16 States have no Class III Senator, a coalition of \(n\) States can elect at most \(n + \min(n,16)\) loyal Senators by early 2003. To control the Senate with a friendly VP tie-break, it needs 50 loyal Senators, so \(n \ge 34\). With 33, max is 49. Thus 25/26 answers fail on timing, not just quorum.

34 suffices if: choose all 16 no-Class III States plus 18 populous States; by Jan. 2003 get 50 loyal Senators + VP; use Article I §5 compulsory attendance to defeat quorum boycott; use Senate majority rule change/nuclear option to defeat filibuster; control House/Presidency. Then admit 14 loyal new States: total 64, coalition 48 = exactly 3/4. Also 48 > 2/3 of 64 for an Article V convention. Ratify by Apr. 2004.

Caveats: if compulsory attendance is disallowed, minimum may be 35 (need 51 loyal Senators for quorum). If filibuster cannot be overcome, statehood route fails for n<44, so direct Article V route gives 38. 38 is sufficient directly: 38 States can apply for a convention (2/3 = 34) and ratify (3/4 of 50 = 38). C3 remains at 25 but ignores Senate class timing. C4/C6 argue 26 on quorum but also ignore class timing. Main unresolved question: whether nuclear option/compulsion are considered cleanly legal under the problem’s “strict legality” constraint. My leaning: 34, with 38 as fallback if controversial Senate procedures are excluded.
}

